      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The neighborhood sits on the east side of the lake and grew between 1885‑1930, with historic homes that now line its tree‑lined streets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.eastisles.org/about-the-neighborhood",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Two remaining islands, Mike’s Island and Raspberry Island, are protected wildlife refuges where virgin woods still thrive.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_of_the_Isles",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Midtown Greenway, a former railroad line, cuts through East Isles and links the neighborhood to the Mississippi River and city limits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.eastisles.org/about-the-neighborhood",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In the 1880s dredging reshaped the lake, using peat and silt to create 36 acres of parkland that surrounds the water.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
